<p>A lock makes one transaction wait for another. That wait is useful when two writers must take turns over the same decision: who gets the last mug, who takes the last class place, or which worker claims a job. It costs time, and it protects only what the transactions actually lock. Two writers can wait politely and still make a decision from the wrong data.</p>
<p>The design therefore has two parts. Find the row or other authority that every competing writer must pass through. Then decide what each writer may read after it gets its turn. The first prevents simultaneous decisions; the second makes each decision account for the one before it.</p>
<p>We use PostgreSQL 18 and the shop’s tea classes. Session S501 has capacity 1 and starts with no bookings. Mina attempts booking B201; Tariq attempts B202. The intended outcome admits Mina and refuses Tariq. The drawings choose a schedule so that we can follow it; separate experiments on PostgreSQL 18.6 check the engine behaviour.</p>
<h2 id="what-a-row-lock-stops">What a row lock stops</h2>
<p>An update takes a row lock automatically. A separate <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code> asks to lock the rows it returns, keeping another transaction from changing or taking an incompatible lock on them until this transaction ends. A plain reader can still read its permitted row version. Locking a product does not make it disappear from the catalogue.</p>
<p>The introduction’s stock update puts the condition and the change in one command. At Read Committed, an overlapping update waits and tests its condition again against the row the first writer committed. For a decision needing several statements, a locking read can hold the row while application code does the rest:</p>
<Sql label="Read a product while reserving its turn" code={`BEGIN;
SELECT stock FROM products
WHERE product_id = 'P7'
FOR UPDATE;
-- Check the returned stock, change it, record the order and lines.
-- Roll back the complete purchase if stock is insufficient.
COMMIT;`} />
<p>The useful scope is the transaction, rather than just the time the select runs. Ending the transaction immediately after reading the stock would release the lock before the purchase used it. A pool helper on another connection would be outside that protection. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/application-sql/">SQL at the application boundary</a> follows connection ownership.</p>
<p><code>FOR UPDATE</code> is the strongest row-lock mode. PostgreSQL also has <code>FOR NO KEY UPDATE</code>, <code>FOR SHARE</code> and <code>FOR KEY SHARE</code>, with different conflicts. For example, a non-key update can coexist with a key-share lock used to protect a referenced key. Choose a mode from the operations it must exclude. Taking a stronger mode than necessary creates extra waiting; taking a weaker one can leave the decision unprotected.</p>
<h2 id="lock-the-authority">Lock the authority, including the empty case</h2>
<p>Class capacity lives across rows. The application counts bookings for S501, compares the count with capacity 1, then inserts one booking if there is room. Locking the matching bookings sounds plausible:</p>
<Sql label="A locking query that cannot protect an empty class" code={`SELECT booking_id FROM bookings
WHERE session_id = 'S501'
FOR UPDATE;`} />
<p>At the start there are no matching rows. Both buyers get an empty result, and neither has locked a booking row that could make the other wait. Each can insert a different booking ID. The booking primary key correctly permits B201 and B202; it does not enforce the class capacity.</p>
<p>Use an authority that exists before the first booking. In this schema variant, <code>sessions</code> has one row per session, with <code>session_id</code> as its primary key and a required <code>capacity</code>. Each booking has a unique <code>booking_id</code>, a reference to its session and its customer ID. The session row is the <em>guard</em>: every operation that can affect the capacity decision takes it first.</p>
<Sql label="Count in a separate statement after acquiring the Read Committed guard" code={`BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED;
SELECT capacity FROM sessions
WHERE session_id = 'S501'
FOR UPDATE;

SELECT count(*) FROM bookings
WHERE session_id = 'S501';
-- Compare that count with the returned capacity.
-- If full: ROLLBACK and return "full".
-- Otherwise, for Mina:
INSERT INTO bookings (booking_id, session_id, customer_id)
VALUES ('B201', 'S501', 1);
COMMIT;`} />
<p>Those are two read statements on purpose. Mina holds S501 while she counts and inserts. Tariq cannot acquire that row until Mina finishes. Once he does, his <em>next</em> statement starts a fresh Read Committed snapshot, which includes Mina’s committed booking. He counts 1 and refuses B202.</p>
<p>Combining the lock and a booking count in one statement would not establish that fresh view after the wait. Nor can a writer count first, wait for the guard and then use its saved count. The protocol is “acquire, then count in a new statement, then decide”. Cancelling a booking, moving it between sessions and changing capacity need compatible protocols too; moving between two sessions requires both authorities in a consistent order.</p>
<h2 id="waiting-and-snapshots">Waiting does not refresh a snapshot</h2>
<p>Repeatable Read changes the result. Suppose Tariq establishes his transaction’s snapshot while S501 is still empty. He waits for Mina’s lock, Mina commits B201, and Tariq acquires S501. Mina only locked that session row; she did not update it. Tariq can acquire the row while retaining his older view of the bookings. His count still returns zero.</p>
<p>Figure 1 keeps the guard and Tariq’s view separate. Follow Read Committed to the final count, then choose the lock-only Repeatable Read protocol. The turn passes in both; the booking set Tariq can count differs.</p>
<Guard />
<p>A lock-only guard is therefore not a recipe detached from isolation. On the disposable server, the Read Committed protocol left one booking. The Repeatable Read schedule with an earlier empty snapshot and the same unchanged guard row left two. Waiting itself was observed in both runs.</p>
<p>One Repeatable Read alternative deliberately updates the guard. Add a required integer <code>guard_epoch</code>, initially 0, to <code>sessions</code>. It is a conflict marker, not capacity or a booking count. Every capacity-changing transaction updates it <em>before</em> counting:</p>
<Sql label="Make Repeatable Read decisions conflict on a changed guard" code={`BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ;
UPDATE sessions
SET guard_epoch = guard_epoch + 1
WHERE session_id = 'S501'
RETURNING capacity;
-- Count bookings, decide and insert in this transaction.
-- On 40001: roll back and repeat the whole operation.
COMMIT;`} />
<p>If Mina’s guard update commits after Tariq’s snapshot, PostgreSQL refuses Tariq’s conflicting update with <code>40001</code>. He must start a new transaction and repeat his reads and decision. In the experiment that retry counted B201. Merely retrying the failed update inside the old transaction would not supply the required new view.</p>
<p>Serializable is another complete choice: make all transactions participating in this rule Serializable, keep their reads and writes inside the transaction, and retry serialization failures. Our separate two-reader, two-insert schedule allowed one booking and rejected the other transaction with <code>40001</code>. Serializable preserves a serial outcome; it does not promise that the first buyer to start wins. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/transaction-boundaries/">Transactions and isolation</a> develops that choice.</p>
<h2 id="contention-is-a-queue">Contention is a queue at an authority</h2>
<p>The session guard deliberately makes bookings for S501 take turns. Bookings for a different session can use a different row. One guard for the whole shop would force unrelated classes into the same queue. A guard per customer would do the opposite: Mina and Tariq could both pass different rows while competing for S501.</p>
<p>A sound lock scope follows the rule’s shared authority. Narrowing it is helpful only while every conflicting decision still meets. If one popular session receives more attempts than its guarded transactions can finish, adding application connections adds waiters. It does not make the session row serve two decisions at once.</p>
<p>Keep the guarded transaction short. Do validation that does not depend on changing database state before it starts. Acquire the needed locks, read and change the data, then finish. Waiting for a user, an email provider or another slow service while holding S501 makes everyone behind the holder wait too. External work can be recorded and performed later, as <a href="/learn/databases/relational/outboxes-and-jobs/">Outboxes and database-backed jobs</a> explains.</p>
<p>Bound the wait as part of the request’s budget. A transaction-local <code>lock_timeout</code> limits each lock acquisition. <code>statement_timeout</code> bounds a statement, including its lock waiting. Leave time for rollback and for the application to return a meaningful result; neither setting is a complete end-to-end request deadline.</p>
<Sql label="Choose operation-specific limits inside the transaction" code={`SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '200ms';
SET LOCAL statement_timeout = '2s';`} />
<p>These durations are examples to choose against your own budget, not measured safe defaults. A lock timeout reports <code>55P03</code>; the application must end or appropriately recover the failed transaction. <code>NOWAIT</code> on a locking read refuses an unavailable row immediately. <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> omits unavailable rows, which is useful for some work queues. It would be wrong for this capacity guard: skipping S501 cannot mean that S501 is empty or that a booking is allowed.</p>
<h2 id="deadlocks">When a queue becomes a cycle</h2>
<p>One waiter and one holder are contention. A deadlock needs a cycle: each transaction is waiting for a lock that another transaction in the cycle holds. A purchase containing several products can create it even if no code explicitly writes <code>FOR UPDATE</code>, because updates take locks too.</p>
<p>Use a separate illustrative two-product schedule. Writer A locks P3, and writer B locks P7. A then asks for P7; B asks for P3. Neither can finish and release its first row while it waits for the second.</p>
<figure class="cycle" aria-labelledby="r14-cycle-title"><figcaption id="r14-cycle-title"><span>Figure 2</span> Two waits close a cycle</figcaption><div class="cycle-row"><div><strong>Writer A</strong><span>holds P3</span><span>wants P7</span></div><p>waits for B <span class="wide">→</span><span class="narrow">↓</span></p><div><strong>Writer B</strong><span>holds P7</span><span>wants P3</span></div></div><p class="return"><span class="wide">←</span><span class="narrow">↑</span> B also waits for A</p><p>Each arrow means “cannot proceed until this other transaction releases its row”. A longer wait alone is not a cycle.</p></figure>
<p>PostgreSQL detects the cycle and aborts a participant with <code>40P01</code>. Which one loses is not an application contract. Roll back that attempt, then rerun the whole transaction if retrying is appropriate. The experiment reproduced this cycle and recorded one deadlock error; it did not assume which writer would receive it.</p>
<p>Prevent this particular cycle by agreeing one order before acquiring any product lock. Both writers take P3 before P7. B then waits for P3 before holding P7, so A can acquire P7 and finish. For variable baskets, sort the distinct product IDs and acquire each row in that order; use the same rule on every path. Do not assume an unordered update over several IDs acquires locks in the order written in the input list.</p>
<p>Order spans tables and helpers too. If one path locks an order before its products and another does the reverse, sorting only the products does not fix their cycle. Foreign-key checks, triggers and lock upgrades can introduce more dependencies. Acquire the needed strength early where possible, make the order explicit, and retain deadlock handling even after removing the known cycle.</p>
<h2 id="read-the-wait">Read the wait before changing the lock</h2>
<p>A slow statement is not enough to identify a blocker. Establish which session is waiting, which session blocks it, and what transaction that holder is keeping open. PostgreSQL’s <code>pg_blocking_pids(pid)</code> gives the immediate blocking sessions; <code>pg_stat_activity</code> supplies their state and transaction start.</p>
<Sql label="Find waiting sessions and their direct blockers" code={`SELECT pid, state, xact_start,
       wait_event_type, wait_event,
       pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blockers
FROM pg_stat_activity
WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) > 0;`} />
<p>Follow the blockers until you reach a holder that can make progress, or find a cycle. An idle transaction can hold the row while doing no current SQL. A queued incompatible request can also block a request behind it. The longest-running query is not necessarily the transaction responsible for the wait.</p>
<p>Use that evidence to change the cause: shorten the holder, remove an unnecessary lock, repair ordering or reduce admissions to an overloaded authority. Cancelling a waiting query does not release the holder’s locks. Cancelling the holder’s statement may still leave its transaction needing rollback. Live intervention and detailed PostgreSQL wait tools belong to <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/operations/">Operating and diagnosing PostgreSQL</a>.</p>
<h2 id="repeat-the-schedules">Repeat the schedules</h2>
<p><a href="/review/relational-collection/evidence/r14/run-locks.py/" download="run-locks.py">Download the lock experiment</a> and run <code>python3 run-locks.py --bindir /path/to/postgresql/bin</code>. It uses the Python standard library and PostgreSQL’s client, creates an owned disposable cluster with a private socket, checks its identity and removes it afterwards. It needs permission to create local sockets and shared memory.</p>
<p>The recorded PostgreSQL 18.6 run checks the empty-booking counterexample, the fresh Read Committed count, the stale Repeatable Read count, a changed-guard conflict and complete retry, a Serializable conflict, a deadlock and a lock timeout. Blocking is coordinated through observed blocking sessions rather than an assumed sleep. These are functional schedules on one local server, not throughput measurements or proof that another engine implements the same isolation names.</p>
<h2 id="sources-and-further-reading">Sources and further reading</h2>
<ul>
<li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html">Explicit Locking</a>: lock conflicts, lifetime, deadlocks and advisory locks. Advisory locks can name an application authority without a row, but every writer must honour them and their scope must fit the connection lifetime.</li>
<li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html">Transaction Isolation</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html">Serialization Failure Handling</a>: snapshots, concurrent guard updates and complete retries.</li>
<li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-select.html#SQL-FOR-UPDATE-SHARE">The locking clause</a>: selected rows, <code>NOWAIT</code> and the incomplete view from <code>SKIP LOCKED</code>.</li>
<li>PostgreSQL 18, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-client.html">Client Connection Defaults</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-info.html">System Information Functions</a>: timeout scope and blocking-session identities.</li>
<li>On this site, <a href="/learn/databases/relational/connections-and-capacity/">Connections, overload and capacity</a> develops queue budgets; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Retries when a commit reply is missing</a> separates a known abort from an uncertain outcome.</li>
</ul>
